WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.560
Have you ever been watching a movie or a TV
show and you saw an actor doing a thing that

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.851
you know a lot about and they are just not
doing it right?

00:00:08.851 --> 00:00:11.939
Well, we have and that thing we know a lot
about is microscopy.

00:00:11.939 --> 00:00:17.050
And there are plenty of examples out there
of movies and TV shows that get microscopy

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.730
right, and then there are others that show
a scientist looking through a microscope that

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.331
has no objectives attached to it.

00:00:24.331 --> 00:00:27.552
And yes, that's a real example of something
we've seen in a TV show.

00:00:27.552 --> 00:00:30.939
But instead of just sitting there, wanting
to shout at the screen, we have decided that,

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:36.210
exclusively for our Patreon patrons, we’re
going to record a Microcosmos Reacts video

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:41.838
where some members of our team will be reacting
to some good and not-so-good movie microscopy.

00:00:41.838 --> 00:00:47.080
But we will only make this video if we hit
our current goal of 900 monthly patrons!

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.250
So, if you would like to support this channel
and help us hit our goal so we can make that

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.877
video, you can head on over to patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:00:55.877 --> 00:01:00.870
And please let us know in the comments below
if you have any favorite movies and TV shows

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.914
that have done microscopy either really well
or really bad.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:35.220
The organism here is one of the more unusual-looking
microbes we’ve seen in the microcosmos.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.362
In fact, calling it an organism seems strange.

00:01:39.362 --> 00:01:44.935
It looks more like a coral reef painted by
an artist inspired by both Gustav Klimt

00:01:44.935 --> 00:01:47.088
 and a pile of trash.

00:01:47.088 --> 00:01:51.170
And if you saw that painting hanging in a 
museum, you might pass it by without thinking

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.996
much of its subject.

00:01:52.996 --> 00:01:58.380
In the middle of the 19th century, an
organism that looked a bit like this one did

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.888
a terrible thing.

00:01:59.888 --> 00:02:03.469
It could not know that it was doing 
something terrible of course.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:08.569
It had recently arrived in a new land, and
all it sought was some food.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:14.390
But in the process, it starved a nation and
killed many people.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:19.780
The organism was a type of water mold called
Phytophthora infestans, which made its

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.888
way from North America to Ireland.

00:02:22.888 --> 00:02:26.528
And with its arrival came the Great Famine.

00:02:26.528 --> 00:02:32.280
Phytophthora infestans infected potato and
tomato plants, producing lesions on the leaves

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.863
and stems, and appearing as a white growth
along the lesions.

00:02:36.863 --> 00:02:40.734
The infected crops rotted, making them inedible.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:50.580
Phytophthora infestans belongs to a group
of organisms called oomycetes, which translates

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.208
to “egg fungus.”

00:02:52.208 --> 00:02:57.080
And we’ve actually encountered another destructive
member of this family before, early in our

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.491
journey through the microcosmos.

00:02:59.491 --> 00:03:04.084
We recommend going and watching that video
because it’s still one of the oddest events

00:03:04.084 --> 00:03:07.156
we’ve watched unfold under a microscope.

00:03:07.156 --> 00:03:13.000
But what we’ve learned since then thanks
in part to oomycete expert Bill Fry from Cornell

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.359
University, is that we were watching a parasitic
oomycete that had managed to turn a Daphnia’s

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:24.111
egg into its own vehicle for reproduction.

00:03:24.111 --> 00:03:30.030
On the rare occasion that we find oomycetes
in our samples, it’s almost always under

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:36.140
similar grim circumstances, where these strange
creatures have found a way to grow on the

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:39.695
bodies of dead bugs or crustaceans.

00:03:39.695 --> 00:03:44.780
And we’re never entirely sure if they just
happened to stumble on a dying organism to

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.739
feed on, or if they were actually the cause
of death.

00:03:50.786 --> 00:03:55.520
Now, there are free-living oomycetes that
don’t infect other organisms.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.762
Instead, they feed on decaying life.

00:03:58.762 --> 00:04:05.300
But as for the rest: an oomycete researcher
named Gordon Beakes once gave a talk titled

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:07.920
“natural born killers”.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.640
And if you go through a list of oomycetes,
you start to realize just how well-earned

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:15.809
that name is.

00:04:15.809 --> 00:04:22.233
There’s Aphanomyces astaci, which has wiped
out populations of European crayfish.

00:04:22.233 --> 00:04:27.133
There’s Phytophthora ramorum, which took
down oak trees in California.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:34.340
There’s grapevine-targeting Plasmopara viticola,
the maize-infecting Peronosclerospora philippinensis,

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:40.200
the list goes on and on, affecting everything
from soybeans to chocolate production.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.778
And none of this sounds that out of character
for a group of organisms collectively called

00:04:44.778 --> 00:04:46.609
“egg fungus,” right?

00:04:46.609 --> 00:04:51.815
There are fungi that like to eat dying things,
and there are fungi that like to infect plants.

00:04:51.815 --> 00:04:56.699
We’ve even witnessed murderous fungi at
work here in the microcosmos, weaving their

00:04:56.699 --> 00:04:59.866
web around unsuspecting nematodes.

00:05:01.007 --> 00:05:07.509
So oomycetes are just one more of these fungi,
their filamentous structures helping them

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:13.023
spread their bodies as they prepare spores
that will help them spread even further.

00:05:13.023 --> 00:05:17.700
Except, oomycetes are not fungi.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:23.360
Their walls are not made of chitin the same way
fungi walls are, and their nuclei have two

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.028
copies of genetic information compared to
the fungi’s singular copy.

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:36.454
Instead, it turns out that oomycetes are members
of a different group of organisms called Stramenopiles,

00:05:36.454 --> 00:05:44.437
making oomycetes more closely related to diatoms
and brown algae than they are to fungi.

00:05:44.437 --> 00:05:47.239
And it’s strange to think about it in these
terms.

00:05:47.239 --> 00:05:52.690
Diatoms are about as close as you can get
to pacifists in the microcosmos.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:58.571
They enclose themselves in silica walls and
photosynthesize to make their own food.

00:05:58.571 --> 00:06:03.830
It’s like oomycetes are killers turning
up to a family reunion full of hippies who

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:06.832
spend their days churning sunshine into sugar.

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:08.931
You can’t help but look at them and ask,

00:06:08.931 --> 00:06:11.370
“How did you turn out like that?”

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.884
Well this certainly wouldn’t 
be the first time two 

00:06:13.884 --> 00:06:18.454
very unrelated organisms share 
an uncanny resemblance.

00:06:18.454 --> 00:06:24.360
Birds and bats and bees all have wings, but
the wings do not make them closely related.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.530
These animals just went through their own
histories of adaptation and survival, and

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.584
in the process converged upon the same idea.

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:38.346
And there’s probably some degree to which
oomycetes and fungi experienced something similar.

00:06:38.346 --> 00:06:44.740
They both happened upon a form and lifestyle
that parasitized and infected, profiting on

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:46.562
the decay that results.

00:06:46.562 --> 00:06:49.780
But at some point, something strange happened.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:57.393
The supposedly-parallel paths of oomycetes
and fungi intersected, sparking collaboration.

00:06:57.393 --> 00:07:01.050
Or rather, oomycetes copied fungi.

00:07:05.108 --> 00:07:10.940
The process by which oomycetes did this is
called horizontal gene transfer, which allows

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:16.858
genetic material to move from one organism
to another through some means that is not

00:07:16.858 --> 00:07:18.187
reproduction.

00:07:18.187 --> 00:07:24.398
It’s pretty common in prokaryotes, but as scientists
have learned through oomycetes, eukaryotes

00:07:24.398 --> 00:07:27.819
can go through horizontal 
gene transfer as well.

00:07:27.819 --> 00:07:34.500
As part of our ongoing battle with oomycetes,
scientists have had to get well-acquainted

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.307
with their genes.

00:07:36.307 --> 00:07:41.670
And as they studied these sequences, they
found that some of the species, particularly

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:49.030
the plant-infecting species, had genes whose
origins had to have come from fungi through

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:51.780
horizontal gene transfer.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:57.210
In Phytophthora ramorum, the oomycete that
kills oak trees, scientists estimated that

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:04.830
7.6% of the proteins released by the organism
are produced from genes that originally came

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.488
from fungi.

00:08:06.488 --> 00:08:12.456
Some of these copied genes have given oomycetes
the ability to break down plant cell walls.

00:08:12.456 --> 00:08:16.620
Others may help the oomycetes combat plant
defenses.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:22.277
Based on our look through the research on
horizontal gene transfer from fungi to oomycetes,

00:08:22.277 --> 00:08:25.883
it’s not clear what the process actually
looks like.

00:08:25.883 --> 00:08:31.530
That information makes horizontal
gene transfer seem like some arcane biological

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:37.360
magic, bringing together two organisms that
superficially resemble each other and making

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:41.272
them related without making them family.

00:08:41.272 --> 00:08:47.234
Meanwhile, in the world around us, spores
spread and take hold of dead insects

00:08:47.234 --> 00:08:50.050
and soon-to-be-dead plants.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:55.950
And buried in their bodies is the evidence
of the unusual collaboration that has made

00:08:55.950 --> 00:09:00.353
them so deadly, an incomplete answer to the
question,

00:09:00.353 --> 00:09:02.711
“How did you get this way?”

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:16.201
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:16.201 --> 00:09:19.430
If you’re interested in fungi that’s a
little less micro, you should check out this

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:25.770
video from our sibling channel PBS Eons, all
about the giant, ancient fungi that once ruled

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:26.960
the world.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.230
And while you’re over there, you should
also subscribe and check out the rest of their

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:35.100
videos, where they will take you on a journey 
through the history of life on Earth.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:40.606
You can find the giant fungi video, and much
much more at youtube.com/eons

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:46.029
The folks on the screen right now, they are
our Patreon patrons.

00:09:46.029 --> 00:09:49.890
They're the people who make it possible for
